## Reasoning

The current solution uses a shortest-path-heuristic (SPH) Steiner tree under 5 fixed destination orderings. Three concrete weaknesses remain:

1. **Fixed orderings only** — SPH quality is highly order-sensitive. Adding a *dynamic best-fit* variant (always attach the globally cheapest-attachable destination next) and *seeded random restarts* explores the search space much better at negligible cost (Dijkstra results are cached).
2. **No post-processing** — the tree is used as-is. A cheap local search that, per destination, tries rerouting onto its direct shortest path when that lowers the *unique-edge union total* (freeing trunk edges used only by that dst) can only improve or tie the winner.
3. **Subtle correctness issue** — `nx.shortest_path(tree, ..., weight="cost")` runs on a tree whose edges only carry a `data` attribute, so networkx silently falls back to hop-count weighting; if the union-of-paths graph contains multiple routes, the extracted path may not be the cheapest in-tree path. Scoring/extracting on an explicit cost-weighted projection fixes this.

I also restructure scoring around *per-dst path plans* (union of unique edges), which makes the hybrid rerouting trivially expressible, and keep everything deterministic (seeded RNG) and well within the time budget (cached Dijkstras, ~20 cheap tree builds).

