## Why this should improve the combined score

- **More candidate trees, better coverage**: dynamic best-fit SPH plus 15 seeded random restarts explore orderings that fixed permutations miss; SPH solution quality varies significantly with order, so this directly lowers the winning tree's unique-edge cost.
- **Hybrid rerouting is monotone**: it only accepts a per-dst switch to a direct path when the union total strictly decreases, so the final plan is never worse than the best tree — it recovers cases where forcing a dst through a shared trunk is more expensive than its direct route.
- **Correct in-tree path extraction**: the cost-weighted projection guarantees each dst gets its cheapest path within the chosen structure (the old hop-count fallback could pick a costlier route when the union graph had alternatives).
- **Safety**: all candidates are scored on the same unique-edge union metric; unreachable dsts fall back to direct Dijkstra paths exactly as before, preserving the 100% success rate. Runtime stays tiny (cached Dijkstras; ~20 cheap tree builds).
